{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2177-09-10 19:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hello, Mr. ___. I’m Dr. ___. You were brought in because of shortness of breath and palpitations. Can you tell me what happened?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2177-09-10 19:16:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, at rehab I started feeling dizzy, my heart was racing, and I couldn’t catch my breath. My oxygen dropped to 74% one morning. They said it might be another pulmonary embolism."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2177-09-10 19:18:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. We reviewed your CT from the ER — no new clots were found. But you’ve had PEs before, so we’re keeping a close eye on that. Your INR was 1.9, which is low, so we’ve restarted the heparin drip for now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2177-09-10 19:20:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been on warfarin — 37.5 mg daily. Why isn’t it working? I’ve read studies about warfarin resistance."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2177-09-10 19:22:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s a good question. We’re looking into that. Hematology is helping us. We checked your Factor II and X levels — they’re low, which suggests you may not have been absorbing or taking the full dose consistently."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2177-09-10 19:24:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I take it every day. Maybe the pharmacy made a mistake?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2177-09-10 19:26:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s possible, but we also saw your INR jumped to 7.9 when we restarted warfarin at 30 mg. That suggests you weren’t on that high a dose regularly. We’ve lowered it to 10 mg and are titrating up carefully."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2177-09-10 19:28:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. What about the breathing episodes? They came on fast — tachycardia, pulsatile tinnitus, oxygen dropping."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2177-09-10 19:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re concerned about that. The episodes didn’t show new clots on imaging. But you mentioned a 28-pound weight gain recently. That, plus your sleep apnea, could be causing flash pulmonary edema."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2177-09-10 19:32:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s heart failure? My echocardiogram was normal last time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2177-09-10 19:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The echo we did was suboptimal due to your body habitus, but no major wall motion issues were seen. LVEF was over 55%. We think fluid overload is playing a role. We’re starting IV furosemide to help you lose some fluid."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2177-09-10 19:36:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve taken oral furosemide before. It doesn’t work as well."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2177-09-10 19:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll try IV first, then transition to a higher oral dose. We’re also restricting fluids and salt. Your weight was 386 lbs on admission — we’re aiming to get you closer to your dry weight."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2177-09-11 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your oxygen levels have been stable overnight on room air. The chest X-ray shows no pulmonary edema. Your CPAP is helping, but staff noticed you sometimes remove it when you fall asleep during the day."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2177-09-11 08:17:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I didn’t realize. I’ll be more careful. Could the hypoxia be from sleep apnea alone?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2177-09-11 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s contributing, especially with your obesity. But the sudden drops during walking suggest more than just apnea. We’re still watching closely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2177-09-12 07:45:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We did a Doppler ultrasound — no DVT in either leg. That’s good news. Your leg wound is being cared for daily. General surgery looked at it and said no further antibiotics or surgery are needed right now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2177-09-12 07:47:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "But there’s still redness. Shouldn’t we get a second opinion? Or consider a plastic surgeon for closure?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2177-09-12 07:50:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The redness is physiologic — part of healing. Granulation tissue looks healthy. We can arrange a plastic surgery consult for the future, but now isn’t the time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2177-09-12 14:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We got your blood culture results back. It grew several organisms — Enterobacter, viridans strep, and Micrococcus. But since only one set was positive and you’re not febrile, we think it’s contamination, likely from the PICC line."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2177-09-12 14:22:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "But I was on antibiotics. Could it be a real infection?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2177-09-12 14:24:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "ID agrees it’s likely contamination. We repeated cultures — they’re negative. We’re stopping vancomycin and Zosyn. Your wound isn’t infected, and we don’t want to promote resistance."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2177-09-12 22:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You had a brief episode last night — became unresponsive for about a minute while a blood gas was being drawn. You recovered quickly. ABG showed normal oxygen and pH. No seizure activity was seen."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2177-09-12 22:12:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I remember someone asking if they were drawing a blood gas. I felt strange, like I blacked out but came back fast."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2177-09-12 22:14:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Neurology reviewed it — no signs of seizure or stroke. Given your history, psychiatry evaluated you for possible factitious disorder. They diagnosed adjustment disorder with anxious features."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2177-09-12 22:16:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m not faking anything. I’m just worried and want to understand my care."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2177-09-12 22:18:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We hear your concerns. But the PICC line was found with a syringe and white residue. For safety, we removed the PICC line. We’ll use peripheral access going forward."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2177-09-13 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your INR is now 3.1 on 20 mg warfarin. That’s improving. We’re keeping you on heparin until it’s consistently therapeutic."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2177-09-13 08:02:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "When can I stop the heparin?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2177-09-13 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Soon, if your INR stays stable. You have follow-up with Dr. ___, a specialist in anticoagulation, next week."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2177-09-14 08:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re down to 375 lbs — great progress. No more hypoxia episodes since starting diuresis. Your heart rhythm shows frequent PVCs, but cardiology says it’s likely benign. They recommend continuing carvedilol."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2177-09-14 08:32:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I still feel palpitations. Could it be VT?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2177-09-14 08:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Telemetry showed what looked like VT, but rhythm strips were inconsistent — likely artifact. You were asymptomatic. No evidence of toxic ingestion. We’ll keep monitoring."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2177-09-15 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your CT abdomen showed no abscess or fistula. Some gallbladder sludge, but nothing requiring action now. Renal cyst noted — likely benign."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2177-09-15 09:02:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Any link to my abdominal discomfort?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2177-09-15 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not clearly. Your symptoms don’t match. We’ll watch it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2177-09-16 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your rheumatologist increased your dexamethasone to 4.5 mg for joint pain. We also restarted azathioprine. Pain management added OxyContin 30 mg twice daily for better control."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2177-09-16 08:17:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been asking for stronger pain relief. Why did it take so long?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2177-09-16 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We needed to ensure it was safe with your anticoagulation. We avoided injections due to bleeding risk. Oral opioids are now appropriate."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2177-09-17 07:50:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your INR is 4.2 — therapeutic. We’ve held warfarin today. You’ll restart at home based on your clinic’s instructions."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2177-09-17 07:52:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So I’m going home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2177-09-17 07:54:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re planning discharge tomorrow if all goes well. You’re at 366 lbs, breathing well, and your wound is healing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2177-09-17 15:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m not sure I’m ready. I can’t climb stairs. And my INR is still high."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2177-09-17 15:32:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your INR is mildly elevated but safe for discharge. Physical therapy says you’re independent with mobility. Home health will assist with wound care and monitoring."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2177-09-17 15:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’d like a second opinion before discharge."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2177-09-17 15:37:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I understand your concern, but your condition is stable, and further delay isn’t medically indicated. We’ve reviewed everything with the team."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2177-09-18 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good news — you’re cleared for discharge today. Your oxygen is 99% on room air, weight is stable, and INR is appropriate."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2177-09-18 09:02:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about my follow-ups?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2177-09-18 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You have appointments with anticoagulation clinic, rheumatology, general surgery for your wound, and psychiatry. We’re sending all instructions to your home team."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2177-09-18 09:06:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my medications?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2177-09-18 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll continue insulin, atorvastatin, lisinopril, carvedilol, dexamethasone, azathioprine, and now oral furosemide 80 mg twice daily. OxyContin for pain, plus Dilaudid as needed. Warfarin will restart per your clinic."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2177-09-18 09:10:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No more IV antibiotics?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2177-09-18 09:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Correct. Your wound is clean, and cultures suggest the positive blood culture was contamination. No need for further antibiotics."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2177-09-18 09:14:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the CPAP?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2177-09-18 09:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Use it every night. It’s crucial for preventing hypoxia. We’ll ensure you have it at home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 56,
      "time": "2177-09-18 09:18:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I just want to stay out of the hospital."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 57,
      "time": "2177-09-18 09:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We all do. Keep up with your follow-ups, take your meds, and call if you have chest pain, severe shortness of breath, or wound changes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 58,
      "time": "2177-09-18 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "One more thing — your wound care: clean daily, use antimicrobial dressing, pack gently, cover with gauze and wrap. Home health will help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 59,
      "time": "2177-09-18 10:02:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll follow it exactly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 60,
      "time": "2177-09-18 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Great. We’ll get your discharge papers ready. Take care, Mr. ___."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 61,
      "time": "2177-09-18 14:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Doctor, before I go — can I see my medical record?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 62,
      "time": "2177-09-18 14:32:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You can request it through medical records. We can’t release it at the bedside, but the team will help you with the process."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 63,
      "time": "2177-09-18 14:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’d like to understand the adjustment disorder diagnosis."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 64,
      "time": "2177-09-18 14:37:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Psychiatry felt your anxiety about your health, combined with the PICC incident and telemetry manipulation, suggests adjustment difficulties. Outpatient therapy can help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 65,
      "time": "2177-09-18 14:40:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I just advocate for myself."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 66,
      "time": "2177-09-18 14:42:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "And that’s important. We want you to be involved. But we also need to ensure your safety and the integrity of your care."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 67,
      "time": "2177-09-18 14:45:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I understand. I’ll follow up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 68,
      "time": "2177-09-18 14:47:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re here to support you. Take care, and we’ll see you in clinic."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2177-09-10 19:02:00",
    "admission_end": "2177-09-28 18:33:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 50-year-old male with morbid obesity, steroid-dependent psoriatic arthritis, insulin-dependent diabetes, and obstructive sleep apnea was admitted from a rehabilitation facility due to recurrent episodes of hypoxemia, palpitations, and concern for pulmonary embolism. Initial evaluation ruled out new clots, but the patient had a subtherapeutic INR and a history of warfarin resistance. He was treated with heparin and carefully transitioned back to warfarin, with hematology input. Hypoxemia episodes were attributed to fluid overload and sleep apnea; he was diuresed from 386 to 366 lbs with improvement. A positive blood culture was deemed contaminated, and antibiotics were discontinued. His left leg wound showed signs of healing without active infection. A brief unresponsive episode prompted ICU evaluation and psychiatry consultation, resulting in a diagnosis of adjustment disorder with anxious features. The PICC line was removed due to safety concerns. Pain management was optimized with OxyContin and azathioprine restart. He was discharged home with services, on therapeutic anticoagulation, with follow-up arranged across multiple specialties.",
    "problems": [
      "Other pulmonary embolism and infarction",
      "Hypoxemia",
      "Bacteremia",
      "Cellulitis and abscess of leg, except foot",
      "Psoriatic arthropathy",
      "Obstructive sleep apnea (adult)(pediatric)",
      "Morbid obesity",
      "Unspecified essential hypertension",
      "Other and unspecified hyperlipidemia",
      "Abnormal coagulation profile",
      "Other premature beats",
      "Adjustment disorder with anxiety",
      "Long-term (current) use of steroids",
      "Long-term (current) use of insulin",
      "Secondary diabetes mellitus without mention of complication, not stated as uncontrolled, or unspecified",
      "Adrenal cortical steroids causing adverse effects in therapeutic use"
    ]
  }
}
